WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.120
Today we are going to see that the&nbsp;
research papers that you see here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:16.960
in this series are real. Here you see NVIDIA’s&nbsp;
game changing videoconferencing AI. So what does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:24.720
this do? Why is this so interesting? How does&nbsp;
this transfer a video of us over the internet?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:33.120
Well, here is a crazy idea. It doesn’t! What?&nbsp;
Transmitting video without transmitting video?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.160
How is that even possible?&nbsp;
Well, now, it is possible!

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.960
What they do in this work, is take only the&nbsp;
first image from the video, and they throw away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:51.920
the entire video afterwards! But before that,&nbsp;
it stores a tiny bit of information from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:59.520
which is, how our head is moving over time,&nbsp;
and how our expressions change. That is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:07.760
absolutely outrageous idea, except the fact that&nbsp;
it works. It not only works, but it works really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.000
well. And because this is an amazing paper,&nbsp;
it does not stop there - it can do even more.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.920
Look at these two previous methods&nbsp;
trying to frontalize the input video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.240
This means that we look to the side a little,&nbsp;
and the algorithm synthesizes a new image of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:33.760
us as if the camera was right in front of us.&nbsp;
That sounds like a science fiction movie, except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.600
that it seems absolutely impossible given how&nbsp;
much these techniques are struggling with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:47.760
task…until we look at the new method. My goodness.&nbsp;
There is some jumpiness in the neck movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.800
in the output video here, and some warping issues&nbsp;
here, but otherwise, very impressive results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.880
Now if you have been holding on to your papers&nbsp;
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.880
previous methods are not some ancient papers&nbsp;
that were published a long time ago. Not at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:15.200
all! Both of them were published within the same&nbsp;
year as the new paper. How amazing is that. Wow.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:23.600
And it could also perform these deepfakes too.&nbsp;
Look! We only need one image of the target person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:29.280
and we can transfer all of our gestures to&nbsp;
them, in a way that is significantly better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.960
than most previous methods. Now, of course,&nbsp;
not even this technique was perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:41.200
it still struggled a great deal in the presence&nbsp;
of occluder objects, but still, just the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:47.120
that this is now possible feels like we are&nbsp;
living in the future. What a time to be alive!

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:54.160
Now, I said that today we are going to&nbsp;
see that this paper is as real as it gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.320
so, what does that mean? Well, today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.800
anyone can try this technique. Yes, just&nbsp;
one year after publishing this paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.520
and it is now available as a demo. The link to&nbsp;
it is available in the video description below.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:19.440
And, get this, some people at NVIDIA are already&nbsp;
using it for their virtual meetings. And by “it”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.880
I mean both the compression engine, where&nbsp;
the previous industry standard compression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:32.960
algorithm could do this when given very little&nbsp;
data. That is not much. But the new technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:40.080
with the same amount of data, can now&nbsp;
do this. That is insanity. Loving it.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.960
And, they also use their own gestures to create&nbsp;
these deepfakes and make virtual characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:53.680
come alive, or to frontalize their videos when&nbsp;
talking to each other. It almost feels like we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.920
are living in a science fiction movie.&nbsp;
And all this is out there for us to use.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.920
Also, these technologies will soon&nbsp;
be part of the NVIDIA Video Codec SDK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:13.280
as the AI Face Codec, which means that it will be&nbsp;
soon deployed to an even wider audience to use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:20.320
These companies are already using it. So,&nbsp;
this is one more amazing example that shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:27.200
that the papers that you see here in Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers are real. Sometimes so real that we can go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.520
from a research paper to a product in just&nbsp;
a year. That is absolutely miraculous.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.360
So, what do you think? What would you use&nbsp;
this for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.040
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

